Answer: Yes, Java allows a static method to be called using an object reference. However, the preferred approach is to call it using the class name because the method belongs to the class.

```java
class Demo {
static void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo d = new Demo();
d.display();
}
}
```

Output: Hello

Answer: A static block is used to initialize static variables and execute one-time setup code. It executes once when the class is initialized by the JVM, before the main() method of that class begins execution.

```java
class Demo {
static int a;
static int b;
static {
a = 100;
b = 200;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(b);
}
}
```

Output: 100 200

Answer: An instance block is used to initialize instance variables when an object is created. It executes before the constructor completes.

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
{
a = 1000;
b = 2000;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo d = new Demo();
System.out.println(d.a);
System.out.println(d.b);
}
}
```

Output: 1000 2000

